WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.050 --> 00:00:06.525
The biopic Bohemian Rhapsody brings the story
of Queen and Freddie Mercury to life on the big screen,

00:00:06.550 --> 00:00:09.550
but how much of what you see in
the movie is the band's real life

00:00:09.575 --> 00:00:11.625
and how much is just fantasy?

00:00:11.650 --> 00:00:13.900
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, it's Jan here,

00:00:13.925 --> 00:00:18.400
and in this video I'm revealing
the six biggest differences between
the Bohemian Rhapsody movie

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:21.400
and the true story of Queen and their charismatic frontman.

00:00:21.450 --> 00:00:25.650
Keep watching to the end to find out how
the movie changed Freddie Mercury's real-life look

00:00:25.675 --> 00:00:28.950
and hit the bell to keep up-to-date with
all my new videos.

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:32.650
According to the movie, Freddie Mercury, or
Farrokh Bulsara as he was then,

00:00:32.675 --> 00:00:37.300
first met Brian May and Roger Taylor
after seeing them perform on stage
with their band Smile.

00:00:37.325 --> 00:00:39.800
That same night, Smile's lead singer Tim Staffell

00:00:39.850 --> 00:00:42.850
decided he'd had enough of the band going nowhere and quit.

00:00:42.850 --> 00:00:46.100
So, Mercury offered his own singing and song-writing services,

00:00:46.125 --> 00:00:48.650
and after a quick demo of his vocal talents,

00:00:48.650 --> 00:00:50.100
May and Taylor agreed.

00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:53.500
In reality, however, things happened rather differently

00:00:53.550 --> 00:00:56.375
as Freddie Mercury and Smile's lead singer Tim Staffell

00:00:56.370 --> 00:00:59.725
were actually friends at Ealing Art College
where they both studied,

00:00:59.720 --> 00:01:05.100
and it was Staffell who introduced Mercury
to Brian May and Roger Taylor in the late 1960s.

00:01:05.175 --> 00:01:10.270
Mercury was keen to be the band's lead singer,
so when Staffell left Smile in 1970,

00:01:10.270 --> 00:01:13.030
Mercury was perfectly positioned to take over.

00:01:13.030 --> 00:01:17.025
The filmmakers probably went
for a more condensed version
of how Queen got together

00:01:17.025 --> 00:01:20.050
as they didn't want to spend
too much time on the band's build-up

00:01:20.075 --> 00:01:22.780
but instead focus on their years as a group.

00:01:22.780 --> 00:01:26.200
By the way, Tim Staffell actually
got back together with May and Taylor

00:01:26.225 --> 00:01:29.700
to re-record Smile's song 'Doing All Right' for the movie.

00:01:29.825 --> 00:01:34.050
In the film, we see Queen's lead guitarist
Brian May showing the rest of the band

00:01:34.050 --> 00:01:39.475
his idea for the classic anthem We Will Rock You
in a recording studio some time in 1980.

00:01:39.525 --> 00:01:44.200
And soon after, Queen are shown playing
the new song on stage at Madison Square Garden.

00:01:44.250 --> 00:01:49.090
Now, while the band did perform We Will Rock You
at that famous New York arena in 1980,

00:01:49.090 --> 00:01:52.525
Brian May actually wrote the iconic song
several years earlier

00:01:52.525 --> 00:01:57.330
and, in fact, it appeared on Queen's album
News of the World in 1977.

00:01:57.330 --> 00:02:00.175
The most visually striking issue with this
scene in the movie

00:02:00.175 --> 00:02:04.375
is that in reality when Queen first performed
We Will Rock You in 1977,

00:02:04.375 --> 00:02:08.040
Freddie Mercury didn't have a moustache and had shoulder-length hair.

00:02:08.050 --> 00:02:11.475
But because the film moves
the song's composition to 1980,

00:02:11.475 --> 00:02:16.250
we see Mercury in the studio with the short hair
and moustache he sported from that time.

00:02:16.325 --> 00:02:21.100
Much is made in the movie of how
Mercury's desire for a solo career
broke the band up.

00:02:21.100 --> 00:02:23.950
However, back in the real world,
according to Brian May,

00:02:23.975 --> 00:02:26.425
"[they] all tried to leave the band more than once",

00:02:26.450 --> 00:02:29.775
and in fact, Mercury wasn't even the first member of Queen

00:02:29.775 --> 00:02:31.325
to release a solo album, 

00:02:31.320 --> 00:02:36.050
that was drummer Roger Taylor
whose album Fun In Space came out in 1981.

00:02:36.100 --> 00:02:40.700
And two years later in 1983,
even though Queen did take a break
from performing live,

00:02:40.725 --> 00:02:43.120
they still recorded a new album together.

00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:46.175
Also during that period Taylor
made a second solo album

00:02:46.225 --> 00:02:48.775
and Brian May released a mini-album too.

00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:53.300
As for Mercury's solo offerings,
he released a number of singles
and two albums over the years, 

00:02:53.325 --> 00:02:56.125
including Barcelona and Mr. Bad Guy.

00:02:56.150 --> 00:02:58.700
In the movie, after the band agree to get back together,

00:02:58.750 --> 00:03:01.150
they say, "we haven't played together in years;

00:03:01.175 --> 00:03:04.775
it's kind of suicide to play again
for the first time in front of millions".

00:03:04.850 --> 00:03:07.675
In real life, that just wasn't the case

00:03:07.670 --> 00:03:10.725
as Live Aid took place in mid-July 1985

00:03:10.750 --> 00:03:12.500
and Queen had actually been on tour together

00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:17.850
from August 1984
to mid-May 1985
with their latest album, The Works.

00:03:17.850 --> 00:03:20.275
I imagine the filmmakers made this big change

00:03:20.275 --> 00:03:23.250
in order to raise the stakes
for the band's Live Aid performance

00:03:23.250 --> 00:03:27.150
and make their show-stealing set
look less like a foregone conclusion

00:03:27.220 --> 00:03:30.050
and more like an up-hill behind-the-scenes struggle.

00:03:30.075 --> 00:03:32.625
One of the benefits
of this change for real-life NQueen

00:03:32.650 --> 00:03:35.975
is that it airbrushes out
some controversial moments in their history,

00:03:35.970 --> 00:03:39.075
such as performances in apartheid-era South Africa

00:03:39.100 --> 00:03:42.410
during a United Nations boycott and a Musicians' Union ban.

00:03:42.410 --> 00:03:45.775
I wouldn't expect
to see something like that
in an official biopic,

00:03:45.775 --> 00:03:49.825
but some reviewers have criticised
the film overall for feeling rather sanitised,

00:03:49.825 --> 00:03:53.225
probably due to the band's heavy involvement as producers.

00:03:53.325 --> 00:03:57.600
In another departure from reality,
the film has Mercury finding out
he has AIDS

00:03:57.625 --> 00:04:02.170
and telling his bandmates about his illness
shortly before Live Aid in mid-1985.

00:04:02.170 --> 00:04:06.525
In the real world,
Mercury was reportedly diagnosed
with AIDS in 1987,

00:04:06.550 --> 00:04:10.670
two years after Live Aid,
and he told his bandmates sometime after that.

00:04:10.680 --> 00:04:13.275
It seems like the filmmakers made this timeline change

00:04:13.270 --> 00:04:16.200
as they wanted to use
the Live Aid concert to frame the film

00:04:16.270 --> 00:04:19.250
and finish the movie on a huge high note for the band,

00:04:19.250 --> 00:04:22.300
while still recognising some of what happened after that.

00:04:22.330 --> 00:04:25.625
By bringing Mercury's diagnosis forward several years,

00:04:25.625 --> 00:04:31.000
the film deliberately dials up the poignancy
of both the lyrics he sang and his performance.

00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:33.875
But the choice to cram so many events into one day,

00:04:33.900 --> 00:04:36.225
from Mercury tracking down his future partner,

00:04:36.220 --> 00:04:37.650
to coming out to his parents,

00:04:37.650 --> 00:04:38.950
to performing at Live Aid,

00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:42.300
turns this sequence into something of an exposition dump.

00:04:42.375 --> 00:04:46.930
Prosthetic teeth, an artificial nose,
and a whole variety of wigs and fake moustaches

00:04:46.930 --> 00:04:52.225
were just some of the items used to help actor
Rami Malek look like Freddie Mercury for the movie.

00:04:52.275 --> 00:04:56.520
One thing the film's make-up and hair department
didn't use though was coloured contacts

00:04:56.520 --> 00:05:00.139
to change Malek's light-coloured eyes
to Mercury's dark ones.

00:05:00.130 --> 00:05:02.325
It's perhaps a little odd that they didn't do that,

00:05:02.325 --> 00:05:04.800
so I wonder if Malek
couldn't wear them for some reason.

00:05:04.800 --> 00:05:07.750
I remember Madonna saying
the coloured contacts she wore for Evita

00:05:07.800 --> 00:05:10.690
were pretty uncomfortable and
hard to get used to, for example.

00:05:10.690 --> 00:05:14.625
Or maybe the Bohemian Rhapsody filmmakers
decided matching Mercury's eye color

00:05:14.625 --> 00:05:16.020
just wasn't a big deal.

00:05:16.020 --> 00:05:18.600
After all, the movie's hair &amp;
make-up designer has said,

00:05:18.620 --> 00:05:24.000
"You don't necessarily want them to look exactly alike,
but you do want to
catch the essence of the person."

00:05:24.100 --> 00:05:24.675
Either way,

00:05:24.670 --> 00:05:27.450
a difference in eye color obviously isn't as important

00:05:27.450 --> 00:05:31.225
as finding an actor who
can bring Mercury to life convincingly on screen;

00:05:31.350 --> 00:05:35.330
and Malek has certainly been getting
great reviews for his role in the film.

00:05:35.550 --> 00:05:38.270
So, what did you think of Bohemian Rhapsody?

00:05:38.270 --> 00:05:41.698
Did any of these changes bother you
and did you spot any others?

